19:Tecd,## The problem: operations spread across every class

You have a stable family of element classes, `Circle`, `Square`, `Triangle`and the requirements keep asking for **new operations** over them: compute **area**, then **export** to SVG, then **render** to screen, then estimate a **cut cost**. The naive move is to add an `area()` method to every shape, then an `export()` to every shape, then a `render()`…

Each new operation forces you to **open and edit every element class**. Worse, unrelated concerns, geometry, serialization, drawing, pricing, all pile up *inside* each shape, so `Circle` ends up knowing about SVG and screen pixels. This is the classic **"expression problem"**: with plain methods it's easy to add a new *type* but painful to add a new *operation* across all existing types.

## A real-world analogy: the tax auditor

Picture a **tax auditor** who visits a portfolio of very different businesses, a **bakery**, a **garage**, a **software studio**. The auditor does a *different calculation* at each one (different deductions, different rules), yet the **businesses don't change** to be audited. They simply **let the auditor in** and hand over their books.

Next year a **safety inspector** shows up instead. Same businesses, brand-new operation, and again the businesses didn't change a line. The *visitor* (auditor / inspector) carries the operation; the *element* (business) just **accepts** the visit. That is the whole pattern.

## How it works

Two roles cooperate:

- Every **Element** exposes one hook: `accept(visitor)`.
- The **Visitor** interface declares one method **per element type**: `visitCircle`, `visitSquare`, …

```
interface Shape   { void accept(Visitor v); }
interface Visitor { void visitCircle(Circle c); void visitSquare(Square s); }

class Circle implements Shape {
    void accept(Visitor v){ v.visitCircle(this); }   // hands itself back, typed
}
class AreaVisitor implements Visitor {
    double visitCircle(Circle c){ return PI * c.r * c.r; }
    double visitSquare(Square s){ return s.side * s.side; }
}
```

A **new operation** is now just a **new Visitor class**, `PerimeterVisitor`, `ExportVisitor`, `RenderVisitor`with **zero edits** to the shapes.

## Double dispatch, the engine

A single virtual call dispatches on **one** runtime type. Visitor needs to pick a method based on **two** types at once: *which shape* × *which operation*. It gets there with **two** virtual calls in a row:

1. `element.accept(v)`first dispatch resolves the **element** type. You land inside `Circle.accept`, so the language now knows "this is a Circle."
2. `v.visitCircle(this)`second dispatch resolves the **operation** (visitor) type, and `this` is already statically typed as `Circle`.

> Two dispatches pick one (shape × operation) cell of the grid, something a single `switch` or one virtual call can't do without giving up type safety.

## The trade-off

| Change | Visitor's answer |
|---|---|
| Add a new **operation** | ✓ **easy**, write one new visitor, touch **zero** element classes |
| Add a new **element type** | ✗ **hard**, every existing visitor must grow a `visitTriangle` |

So Visitor is **open for new operations, closed for new types**, the exact mirror of plain methods.

## When to use it / when to avoid

- **Use it** when the element set is **stable** but operations keep multiplying (AST walkers, compilers, document/DOM exporters, report generators, file-format converters).
- **Avoid it** when new *element types* appear often, you'll pay the "edit every visitor" tax constantly, or when there's only one operation (a plain method is simpler).

> **Rule of thumb:** reach for Visitor when *types are few and frozen but operations keep growing*. If it's the other way round, plain polymorphic methods win.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-behavioral","activeLesson":"visitor"}],["$","$L18","visitor",{"lesson":{"id":"visitor","patternId":"lld-behavioral","title":"Visitor","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"Advanced","statement":"Add new operations to a set of object types WITHOUT editing those types, by moving each operation into a visitor and letting elements \"accept\" it.","companies":["Amazon","Google","Oracle"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"Double dispatch","code":["interface Shape   { void accept(Visitor v); }","interface Visitor { void visitCircle(Circle c); void visitSquare(Square s); }","class Circle implements Shape { void accept(Visitor v){ v.visitCircle(this); } }","class AreaVisitor implements Visitor { double visitCircle(Circle c){...} }"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Shape declares a single method: accept(visitor). Circle and Square implement it, that one hook is all an element ever needs. No operation logic lives here.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"el","name":"Shape","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ accept(v)"],"x":0.3,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"circle","name":"Circle","methods":["+ accept(v)"],"x":0,"y":1.6},{"id":"square","name":"Square","methods":["+ accept(v)"],"x":1,"y":1.6},{"id":"vis","name":"Visitor","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ visitCircle(c)","+ visitSquare(s)"],"x":2.3,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent2"},{"id":"area","name":"AreaVisitor","x":2.2,"y":1.6}],"relations":[{"id":"rc","from":"circle","to":"el","kind":"implement"},{"id":"rs","from":"square","to":"el","kind":"implement"},{"id":"ra","from":"area","to":"vis","kind":"implement"},{"id":"rd","from":"circle","to":"vis","kind":"depend","label":"accept→visit"}],"shownClassIds":["el","circle","square"],"shownRelIds":["rc","rs"],"activeIds":["el"]},"vars":[["element","just accept(v)"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"The Visitor interface declares one visit-method per shape type. AreaVisitor implements them, computing the area for each shape, the operation now lives entirely OUTSIDE the shapes.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["el","circle","square","vis","area"],"shownRelIds":["rc","rs","ra"],"activeIds":["vis","area"]},"vars":[["operation","lives in the visitor"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"DOUBLE DISPATCH, call one. The client calls circle.accept(areaVisitor), the FIRST dispatch resolves the ELEMENT type. We are now inside Circle.accept, so the language knows \"this is a Circle\".","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"c","label":"Client","kind":"actor"},{"id":"s","label":"Circle"},{"id":"v","label":"AreaVisitor","tint":"accent2"}],"messages":[{"id":"m1","from":"c","to":"s","label":"accept(areaVisitor)","kind":"call"},{"id":"m2","from":"s","to":"v","label":"visitCircle(this)","kind":"call"},{"id":"m3","from":"v","to":"v","label":"reads radius","kind":"self"},{"id":"m4","from":"v","to":"c","label":"78.5","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":1,"activeIndex":0},"vars":[["dispatch 1","accept() → picks the shape"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"Call two. Inside accept, Circle calls v.visitCircle(this), the SECOND dispatch resolves the OPERATION (visitor) type, with this already typed as Circle. Two virtual calls picked the exact (Circle × area) method.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:messages","shownCount":3,"activeIndex":1},"vars":[["dispatch 2","visitCircle() → picks the op"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":4,"narration":"visitCircle reads the radius and returns 78.5 to the client. accept() chose the shape; visitCircle() chose the operation, neither alone could; together they hit one cell of the (shape × operation) grid.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:messages","shownCount":4,"activeIndex":3},"vars":[["result","78.5"],["why two","one call = one type only"]]}]},{"label":"Open for new operations","code":["// + new OPERATION = one new class, ZERO shapes touched","class PerimeterVisitor implements Visitor {","    double visitCircle(Circle c){ return 2*PI*c.r; }","    double visitSquare(Square s){ return 4*s.side; }","}","","// + new ELEMENT (Triangle) = EVERY visitor must grow a method","interface Visitor { ...; void visitTriangle(Triangle t); }"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Start from the stable side: two shapes (Circle, Square) and an AreaVisitor that already computes their areas. The shapes are frozen, we will NOT touch them again.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"vis","name":"Visitor","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ visitCircle(c)","+ visitSquare(s)"],"x":0.6,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent2"},{"id":"area","name":"AreaVisitor","x":0,"y":1.6},{"id":"perim","name":"PerimVisitor","x":1.3,"y":1.6,"tint":"green"},{"id":"circle","name":"Circle","x":2.5,"y":0.2},{"id":"square","name":"Square","x":2.5,"y":1},{"id":"tri","name":"Triangle","x":2.5,"y":1.8,"tint":"red"}],"relations":[{"id":"oa","from":"area","to":"vis","kind":"implement"},{"id":"op","from":"perim","to":"vis","kind":"implement"}],"shownClassIds":["vis","area","circle","square"],"shownRelIds":["oa"],"activeIds":["area"],"note":"$undefined"},"vars":[["shapes","frozen ✓"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"A new requirement: compute PERIMETERS. Add ONE class: PerimeterVisitor implements the same Visitor interface. That is the entire change: a brand-new operation touching ZERO element classes.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["vis","area","perim","circle","square"],"shownRelIds":["oa","op"],"activeIds":["perim"],"note":"new operation = 1 new class, 0 shapes edited"},"vars":[["new operation","= new Visitor ✓"],["shapes edited","0"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":7,"narration":"Now the OTHER axis: a new ELEMENT type, Triangle (red). Because every visitor enumerates the shapes, the Visitor interface needs visitTriangle, and AreaVisitor AND PerimeterVisitor must BOTH implement it. One new type, edits rippling through every visitor.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["vis","area","perim","circle","square","tri"],"shownRelIds":["oa","op"],"activeIds":["tri","area","perim"],"note":"new shape = edit EVERY visitor 💥"},"vars":[["new shape","edit every visitor 💥"],["files touched","Visitor + N visitors"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"That asymmetry IS the pattern. Visitor is OPEN for new operations (one new class) but CLOSED for new types (touch them all). Choose it only when your element set is stable and the operations are what keep multiplying.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["vis","area","perim","circle","square","tri"],"shownRelIds":["oa","op"],"activeIds":[],"note":"$undefined"},"vars":[["+ operation","easy ✓"],["+ element","hard ✗"]]}]}]}}]]}]
